- A qualified trust service provider must verify the identity (and any specific attributes) of the person to whom the qualified certificate is issued, by appropriate means and in accordance with national law; the provider may verify directly or rely on a third party (E Art. 24.1).

- Permitted verification methods are (E Art. 24.1):
  - physical presence of the natural person (or an authorised representative) (E Art. 24.1(a));
  - remote verification using electronic identification means, for which, prior to issuance, a physical presence was ensured and which meets the requirements in Article 8 for assurance level "substantial" or "high" (E Art. 24.1(b); E Art. 8);
  - by means of a certificate of a qualified electronic signature or qualified electronic seal issued in compliance with point (a) or (b) (E Art. 24.1(c));
  - other identification methods recognised at national level that provide assurance equivalent to physical presence, with that equivalent assurance confirmed by a conformity assessment body (E Art. 24.1(d)).

- Core requirement: keep a record of processing activities under your responsibility (controller) or carried out on behalf of a controller (processor) (Art. 30.1–2).

- If you are a controller, the record must contain all of the following:
  - name and contact details of the controller and, where applicable, joint controller, controller's representative and data protection officer (Art. 30.1(a));
  - the purposes of the processing (Art. 30.1(b));
  - a description of the categories of data subjects and of the categories of personal data (Art. 30.1(c));
  - the categories of recipients to whom the personal data have been or will be disclosed, including recipients in third countries or international organisations (Art. 30.1(d));
  - where applicable, transfers of personal data to a third country or an international organisation, including identification of that country or organisation and, where applicable, the envisaged time limits for erasure of the different categories of data (Art. 30.1(e)–(f));
  - where possible, a general description of the technical and organisational security measures referred to in Article 32 (Art. 30.1(g); see also Art. 32).

- If you are a processor, the record must contain:
  - name and contact details of the processor(s) and of each controller on behalf of which the processor is acting, and, where applicable, of the controller's or the processor's representative, and the data protection officer (Art. 30.2(a));
  - the categories of processing carried out on behalf of each controller (Art. 30.2(b));
  - where applicable, transfers of personal data to a third country or an international organisation, including identification of that country or organisation and, where possible, a general description of the technical and organisational security measures referred to in Article 32 (Art. 30.2(c); see also Art. 32).

- Availability and purpose:
  - the controller or processor must make the record available to the supervisory authority on request (Art. 30.4);
  - maintaining such records serves to demonstrate compliance with the Regulation (Rec. 82) and supports transparency and appropriate security measures (Rec. 39; Rec. 78; Art. 32).

(If you need a template or examples, that is not provided in the supplied context.)

- Purpose: codes of conduct may specify the application of the Regulation (G Art. 40(2)).

- Elements that such a code should/may specify (G Art. 40(2)(a)–(k)):
  - fair and transparent processing (G Art. 40(2)(a));
  - the legitimate interests pursued by controllers in specific contexts (G Art. 40(2)(b));
  - the collection of personal data (G Art. 40(2)(c));
  - the pseudonymisation of personal data (G Art. 40(2)(d));
  - the information provided to the public and to data subjects (G Art. 40(2)(e));
  - the exercise of the rights of data subjects (G Art. 40(2)(f));
  - the information provided to, and the protection of, children, and how parental consent is to be obtained (G Art. 40(2)(g));
  - the measures and procedures referred to in Articles 24 and 25 and the measures to ensure security of processing referred to in Article 32 (G Art. 40(2)(h));
  - the notification of personal data breaches to supervisory authorities and the communication of such breaches to data subjects (G Art. 40(2)(i));
  - the transfer of personal data to third countries or international organisations (G Art. 40(2)(j));
  - out-of-court proceedings and other dispute‑resolution procedures between controllers and data subjects (without prejudice to Articles 77 and 79) (G Art. 40(2)(k)).

- Other relevant points:
  - Codes should take account of the specific features of processing sectors and the specific needs of micro, small and medium‑sized enterprises (G Art. 40(1)).
  - When drawing up, amending or extending a code, associations and bodies should consult relevant stakeholders, including data subjects where feasible, and have regard to submissions and views received (G Rec. 99).
